+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013f2YxM2yzDfkqNF47QhiKo","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Room recreation is handled correctly. I have enough to write up the review.\n\n## Review Summary\n\nThe implementation is well-structured and genuinely good: clean module separation, correct parameterised SQL throughout, proper transactions, sound OT transform rules, and thoughtful touches (socket tracking for shutdown, `RoomSync` interface decoupling REST from WS, schema path resolution with fallbacks). Below are the real issues I could verify, several of which the 45 tests do not exercise.\n\n---\n\n### HIGH\n\n**1. Use-after-close crash on shutdown — uncaught exception per connected client**\n`/workspace/src/server.ts:82-90` and `/workspace/src/ws.ts:84-87`\n\n`collab.closeAll()` calls `client.terminate()`, but `ws` emits the `'close'` event **asynchronously** (via `emitClose` on a stream finish tick). `finish()` then runs `db.close()` synchronously in the same turn. When the deferred `'close'` handlers fire, they call `unregisterConnection(this.db, ...)`, which throws.\n\nVerified: stopping a server with 5 live clients produced 5 uncaught exceptions:\n\n```\nTypeError: The database connection is not open\n    at unregisterConnection (dist/db.js:90:8)\n    at WebSocket.<anonymous> (dist/ws.js:53:13)\n    at WebSocket.emitClose (ws/lib/websocket.js:273:10)\n```\n\nThe tests miss this because they close all clients before `stop()` and vitest does not fail on post-teardown uncaught exceptions. In production this crashes the process on every shutdown that has live sockets.\n\nFix — add a shutdown guard in `CollabServer` so close handlers become no-ops after teardown:\n\n```ts\n// ws.ts\nprivate closing = false;\n\ncloseAll(): void {\n  this.closing = true;   // set BEFORE terminating\n  ...\n}\n\n// in handleConnection's close handler:\nws.on('close', () => {\n  room.clients.delete(ws);\n  if (this.closing || !this.db.open) return;\n  unregisterConnection(this.db, connectionId);\n});\n```\n\nGuarding on `this.db.open` alone would also work and is a cheap belt-and-braces addition inside `registerConnection`/`unregisterConnection` in `/workspace/src/db.ts:124-130`.\n\n**2. `refresh()` creates a history gap that is silently ignored, corrupting later operations**\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:162-165` and `/workspace/src/ws.ts:221-223`\n\n`refresh()` writes `null` into `room.history` to mark \"a change that cannot be transformed through\" (per the comment at `ws.ts:17-20`). But `handleMessage` does `.filter((op): op is Operation => op !== null)` — it drops the sentinel and transforms as if nothing happened, applying a stale-based operation to unrelated content.\n\nVerified: doc `\"\"` → client inserts `\"AB\"` (v1) → `PUT content:\"HELLO\"` (v2) → client sends `{insert, position:2, text:\"!\"}` with `version:1`. The op was composed against `\"AB\"` (position 2 = end of \"AB\") but was applied verbatim to `\"HELLO\"`, yielding `\"HE!LLO\"`. It was acked as success.\n\nFix — detect the gap and force a resync instead of transforming through it:\n\n```ts\nconst slice = room.history.slice(baseVersion, room.version);\nif (slice.some((op) => op === null)) {\n  send(ws, { type: 'sync', documentId: room.documentId, content: room.content, version: room.version });\n  return;\n}\nconst concurrent = slice as Operation[];\n```\n\nThis is safe and clients already handle `sync`. Alternatively reject with an `error` telling the client to re-sync — but `sync` is friendlier and matches the existing protocol.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n**3. Connection ids collide across processes sharing one DB file, and startup wipes another instance's live rows**\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:79` (`conn-${++this.connectionCounter}`) and `/workspace/src/db.ts:37` (`DELETE FROM active_connections`)\n\nThe counter is per-`CollabServer`, so two processes (or two `createApp()` calls) against the same DB file both mint `conn-1`, `conn-2`, …. `registerConnection` uses `INSERT OR REPLACE` (`db.ts:125`), so instance B's `conn-1` silently evicts instance A's row; A's later `unregisterConnection` then deletes B's row. Worse, `createDatabase` unconditionally runs `DELETE FROM active_connections` on open.\n\nVerified: with app1 holding a live connection on a shared DB file, `createApp()` for app2 emptied `active_connections` while app1's client was still connected.\n\nThe spec's \"cleared on startup\" is satisfied for the single-process case the tests cover, but the table is documented as process-scoped while the storage is file-scoped. Fix: use `randomUUID()` for the connection id (`ws.ts:79`) so ids are globally unique, and use plain `INSERT` rather than `INSERT OR REPLACE` so a collision surfaces instead of corrupting. The startup `DELETE` is acceptable for single-process deployment but deserves a comment noting it is unsafe if multiple processes share a DB file.\n\n**4. `listen` callbacks accumulate on repeated failed `start()`**\n`/workspace/src/server.ts:64-70`\n\n`server.once('error', onError)` is correctly removed on success and self-removes on error, but `server.listen(port, cb)` registers a `'listening'` listener on every call and it is never removed when the attempt fails. Verified: 12 failed `start()` calls produced `MaxListenersExceededWarning: 11 listening listeners added to [Server]`.\n\nFix — register the success handler with `once` and remove it in the error path:\n\n```ts\nconst onError = (err: Error) => { server.removeListener('listening', onListening); reject(err); };\nconst onListening = () => {\n  server.removeListener('error', onError);\n  listening = true;\n  resolve((server.address() as AddressInfo).port);\n};\nserver.once('error', onError);\nserver.once('listening', onListening);\nserver.listen(port);\n```\n\n**5. Prepared statements are re-prepared on every call**\n`/workspace/src/db.ts:45-130` (every function calls `db.prepare(...)` inline)\n\n`better-sqlite3` does not cache statements. Measured: 20k `prepare()` calls ≈ 35 ms vs 20k reuses of a cached statement ≈ 4.5 ms — roughly 8x overhead. On the hot path this compounds: each committed operation runs `commitVersion` (2 prepares inside a transaction) plus `getDocument`, and `loadRoom`/`latestVersion` call `listVersions` which prepares and then materialises **every** version row.\n\nFix — lazily memoise statements per database handle:\n\n```ts\nconst cache = new WeakMap<DatabaseHandle, Map<string, Database.Statement>>();\nfunction stmt(db: DatabaseHandle, sql: string): Database.Statement {\n  let m = cache.get(db);\n  if (!m) { m = new Map(); cache.set(db, m); }\n  let s = m.get(sql);\n  if (!s) { s = db.prepare(sql); m.set(sql, s); }\n  return s;\n}\n```\n\nNote the `WeakMap` keying matters: caching on a module-level `Map` keyed by SQL alone would hand statements from a closed DB to a new one.\n\n**6. `latestVersion()` loads the whole version history to read one number**\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:242-245`\n\nIt calls `listVersions()` and takes the last row, materialising every version of the document. `getLatestVersion()` in `/workspace/src/db.ts:86-91` already does this with `SELECT MAX(version)`. Replace the body with `return getLatestVersion(this.db, documentId);` and drop the duplicate. (`loadRoom` at `ws.ts:102` legitimately needs the full scan.)\n\n---\n\n### LOW\n\n**7. `Number()` coercion accepts non-decimal version strings**\n`/workspace/src/routes.ts:119-123`\n\n`Number(req.params.version)` accepts hex and whitespace. Verified: `GET /docs/:id/versions/0x0` → 200 (returns version 0), and `/versions/%20` → 200. `\"1e2\"` becomes 100. The `Number.isInteger` guard catches `abc`/`1.5`/`-1` but not these. Fix: validate the raw string first — `if (!/^\\d+$/.test(req.params.version))` → 400.\n\n**8. Document ids containing `/` are accepted but not addressable by plain path**\n`/workspace/src/routes.ts:42`\n\n`POST /docs {id: \"a/b\"}` returns 201, but `GET /docs/a/b` → 404 (it is only reachable as `/docs/a%2Fb`). Ids are also unbounded in length (a 300-char id was accepted). Not a security or data-loss issue since the storage is parameterised, but worth a format guard, e.g. reject ids not matching `/^[A-Za-z0-9._~-]{1,128}$/`.\n\n**9. `db.pragma('foreign_keys = ON')` is applied before schema creation but journal mode is left at default**\n`/workspace/src/db.ts:34-37`\n\nForeign keys are correctly enabled (verified `foreign_keys: 1`), and `ON DELETE CASCADE` works — deleting a document removed its `versions` and `active_connections` rows in my test. Durability is also fine: rows were visible from a second connection before `stop()` (`journal_mode: delete`, `synchronous: 2`). Consider `db.pragma('journal_mode = WAL')` for concurrent read throughput, but this is a tuning choice, not a correctness bug.\n\n**10. `stop()` sets `listening = false` but leaves `closed = true` permanently**\n`/workspace/src/server.ts:88-90`\n\nAfter `stop()`, `listening` is reset to `false` — implying restartability — but `closed` stays `true` and the db is closed, so a subsequent `start()` would bind a listener against a closed database and fail at the first query. Either drop the `listening = false` reset (the instance is genuinely single-use) or make `start()` throw a clear error when `closed` is true.\n\n---\n\n### Things I checked that are correct\n\n- **Express error-handler ordering** — the 404 catch-all at `/workspace/src/server.ts:35-37` placed *before* the error handler at `:38-40` is **not** a bug. Express dispatches error middleware by arity and skips non-error middleware once an error is in flight. Verified: malformed JSON body → 400 with the parse message; unknown path → 404. No change needed.\n- **SQL injection** — every statement is parameterised; the only non-parameterised query (`/workspace/src/db.ts:50`) has no interpolation.\n- **Transactions** — `insertDocument` (`db.ts:55-62`) and `commitVersion` (`db.ts:104-116`) correctly wrap multi-statement writes; no nesting problems since neither calls the other.\n- **Schema path resolution** — verified `dist/db.js` loads successfully with `cwd=/tmp`, so both the vitest (`src/`) and compiled (`dist/`) cases resolve.\n- **Persistence across restart** — documents, content and version history all survive; data is flushed without needing `stop()`.\n- **DELETE while clients connected** — cascade removes `versions` and `active_connections`, connected clients get `{type:'error', message:'Document was deleted'}` and are closed, and re-creating the same id afterwards yields a clean room (verified).\n- **PUT content overwrite** — correctly skipped when content is unchanged (no spurious version), pushes `sync` to live clients, and a stale out-of-bounds op afterwards is rejected rather than corrupting (issue 2 above is the narrower in-bounds case).\n- **`stop()` resolution** — resolves promptly (0 ms with 5 live sockets); the `!server.listening` short-circuit at `server.ts:93-96` correctly handles stop-without-start, and double-`stop()` is a safe no-op.\n\n### Recommended fix order\nIssues 1 and 2 are the ones I would block on — 1 crashes the process on any shutdown with live connections, and 2 silently corrupts document content while reporting success. Both are small, contained changes in `/workspace/src/ws.ts`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7dcb03ba9c733cfe (use SendMessage with to: 'a7dcb03ba9c733cfe' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 52024\ntool_uses: 31\nduration_ms: 201238</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e246dbcb-e9b4-4717-8ce2-8446dd5eb5a5","uuid":"a1d8fad1-3903-4f7d-a83d-0254f4a25e26","timestamp":"2026-07-25T01:22:44.622Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review the implementation in /workspace/src/ (files: db.ts, models.ts, ot.ts, routes.ts, ws.ts, server.ts) against the spec in /workspace/TASK.md, /workspace/schema.sql and the tests in /workspace/tests/collab.test.ts.\n\nFocus specifically on:\n- REST API correctness in src/routes.ts: status codes, input validation, JSON error handling, SQL injection risk, express error-handler wiring (is the 404 catch-all placed before the error handler a problem?).\n- Database layer src/db.ts: schema application, transactions, foreign keys / cascade behaviour, prepared-statement reuse and performance, schema.sql path resolution from both src/ (vitest) and dist/ (compiled).\n- Persistence semantics: does data survive restart? Is `active_connections` correctly maintained (insert on connect, delete on disconnect, cleared on startup)?\n- src/server.ts lifecycle: does stop() reliably resolve? Any chance of double-close, hanging sockets, use-after-close of the db (e.g. a websocket 'close' event firing after db.close() and throwing)? Any listener leaks?\n- Consistency between the REST layer and the in-memory WebSocket room cache (PUT content overwrite, DELETE while clients are connected).\n\nDo NOT run the test suite (it already passes, 45/45). Read carefully and reason about edge cases the tests do not cover. Report findings as HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW priority with file:line references and concrete recommended fixes. Be specific and skeptical; do not invent problems that aren't real.","agentId":"a7dcb03ba9c733cfe","agentType":"conclave:code-reviewer","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Room recreation is handled correctly. I have enough to write up the review.\n\n## Review Summary\n\nThe implementation is well-structured and genuinely good: clean module separation, correct parameterised SQL throughout, proper transactions, sound OT transform rules, and thoughtful touches (socket tracking for shutdown, `RoomSync` interface decoupling REST from WS, schema path resolution with fallbacks). Below are the real issues I could verify, several of which the 45 tests do not exercise.\n\n---\n\n### HIGH\n\n**1. Use-after-close crash on shutdown — uncaught exception per connected client**\n`/workspace/src/server.ts:82-90` and `/workspace/src/ws.ts:84-87`\n\n`collab.closeAll()` calls `client.terminate()`, but `ws` emits the `'close'` event **asynchronously** (via `emitClose` on a stream finish tick). `finish()` then runs `db.close()` synchronously in the same turn. When the deferred `'close'` handlers fire, they call `unregisterConnection(this.db, ...)`, which throws.\n\nVerified: stopping a server with 5 live clients produced 5 uncaught exceptions:\n\n```\nTypeError: The database connection is not open\n    at unregisterConnection (dist/db.js:90:8)\n    at WebSocket.<anonymous> (dist/ws.js:53:13)\n    at WebSocket.emitClose (ws/lib/websocket.js:273:10)\n```\n\nThe tests miss this because they close all clients before `stop()` and vitest does not fail on post-teardown uncaught exceptions. In production this crashes the process on every shutdown that has live sockets.\n\nFix — add a shutdown guard in `CollabServer` so close handlers become no-ops after teardown:\n\n```ts\n// ws.ts\nprivate closing = false;\n\ncloseAll(): void {\n  this.closing = true;   // set BEFORE terminating\n  ...\n}\n\n// in handleConnection's close handler:\nws.on('close', () => {\n  room.clients.delete(ws);\n  if (this.closing || !this.db.open) return;\n  unregisterConnection(this.db, connectionId);\n});\n```\n\nGuarding on `this.db.open` alone would also work and is a cheap belt-and-braces addition inside `registerConnection`/`unregisterConnection` in `/workspace/src/db.ts:124-130`.\n\n**2. `refresh()` creates a history gap that is silently ignored, corrupting later operations**\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:162-165` and `/workspace/src/ws.ts:221-223`\n\n`refresh()` writes `null` into `room.history` to mark \"a change that cannot be transformed through\" (per the comment at `ws.ts:17-20`). But `handleMessage` does `.filter((op): op is Operation => op !== null)` — it drops the sentinel and transforms as if nothing happened, applying a stale-based operation to unrelated content.\n\nVerified: doc `\"\"` → client inserts `\"AB\"` (v1) → `PUT content:\"HELLO\"` (v2) → client sends `{insert, position:2, text:\"!\"}` with `version:1`. The op was composed against `\"AB\"` (position 2 = end of \"AB\") but was applied verbatim to `\"HELLO\"`, yielding `\"HE!LLO\"`. It was acked as success.\n\nFix — detect the gap and force a resync instead of transforming through it:\n\n```ts\nconst slice = room.history.slice(baseVersion, room.version);\nif (slice.some((op) => op === null)) {\n  send(ws, { type: 'sync', documentId: room.documentId, content: room.content, version: room.version });\n  return;\n}\nconst concurrent = slice as Operation[];\n```\n\nThis is safe and clients already handle `sync`. Alternatively reject with an `error` telling the client to re-sync — but `sync` is friendlier and matches the existing protocol.\n\n---\n\n### MEDIUM\n\n**3. Connection ids collide across processes sharing one DB file, and startup wipes another instance's live rows**\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:79` (`conn-${++this.connectionCounter}`) and `/workspace/src/db.ts:37` (`DELETE FROM active_connections`)\n\nThe counter is per-`CollabServer`, so two processes (or two `createApp()` calls) against the same DB file both mint `conn-1`, `conn-2`, …. `registerConnection` uses `INSERT OR REPLACE` (`db.ts:125`), so instance B's `conn-1` silently evicts instance A's row; A's later `unregisterConnection` then deletes B's row. Worse, `createDatabase` unconditionally runs `DELETE FROM active_connections` on open.\n\nVerified: with app1 holding a live connection on a shared DB file, `createApp()` for app2 emptied `active_connections` while app1's client was still connected.\n\nThe spec's \"cleared on startup\" is satisfied for the single-process case the tests cover, but the table is documented as process-scoped while the storage is file-scoped. Fix: use `randomUUID()` for the connection id (`ws.ts:79`) so ids are globally unique, and use plain `INSERT` rather than `INSERT OR REPLACE` so a collision surfaces instead of corrupting. The startup `DELETE` is acceptable for single-process deployment but deserves a comment noting it is unsafe if multiple processes share a DB file.\n\n**4. `listen` callbacks accumulate on repeated failed `start()`**\n`/workspace/src/server.ts:64-70`\n\n`server.once('error', onError)` is correctly removed on success and self-removes on error, but `server.listen(port, cb)` registers a `'listening'` listener on every call and it is never removed when the attempt fails. Verified: 12 failed `start()` calls produced `MaxListenersExceededWarning: 11 listening listeners added to [Server]`.\n\nFix — register the success handler with `once` and remove it in the error path:\n\n```ts\nconst onError = (err: Error) => { server.removeListener('listening', onListening); reject(err); };\nconst onListening = () => {\n  server.removeListener('error', onError);\n  listening = true;\n  resolve((server.address() as AddressInfo).port);\n};\nserver.once('error', onError);\nserver.once('listening', onListening);\nserver.listen(port);\n```\n\n**5. Prepared statements are re-prepared on every call**\n`/workspace/src/db.ts:45-130` (every function calls `db.prepare(...)` inline)\n\n`better-sqlite3` does not cache statements. Measured: 20k `prepare()` calls ≈ 35 ms vs 20k reuses of a cached statement ≈ 4.5 ms — roughly 8x overhead. On the hot path this compounds: each committed operation runs `commitVersion` (2 prepares inside a transaction) plus `getDocument`, and `loadRoom`/`latestVersion` call `listVersions` which prepares and then materialises **every** version row.\n\nFix — lazily memoise statements per database handle:\n\n```ts\nconst cache = new WeakMap<DatabaseHandle, Map<string, Database.Statement>>();\nfunction stmt(db: DatabaseHandle, sql: string): Database.Statement {\n  let m = cache.get(db);\n  if (!m) { m = new Map(); cache.set(db, m); }\n  let s = m.get(sql);\n  if (!s) { s = db.prepare(sql); m.set(sql, s); }\n  return s;\n}\n```\n\nNote the `WeakMap` keying matters: caching on a module-level `Map` keyed by SQL alone would hand statements from a closed DB to a new one.\n\n**6. `latestVersion()` loads the whole version history to read one number**\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:242-245`\n\nIt calls `listVersions()` and takes the last row, materialising every version of the document. `getLatestVersion()` in `/workspace/src/db.ts:86-91` already does this with `SELECT MAX(version)`. Replace the body with `return getLatestVersion(this.db, documentId);` and drop the duplicate. (`loadRoom` at `ws.ts:102` legitimately needs the full scan.)\n\n---\n\n### LOW\n\n**7. `Number()` coercion accepts non-decimal version strings**\n`/workspace/src/routes.ts:119-123`\n\n`Number(req.params.version)` accepts hex and whitespace. Verified: `GET /docs/:id/versions/0x0` → 200 (returns version 0), and `/versions/%20` → 200. `\"1e2\"` becomes 100. The `Number.isInteger` guard catches `abc`/`1.5`/`-1` but not these. Fix: validate the raw string first — `if (!/^\\d+$/.test(req.params.version))` → 400.\n\n**8. Document ids containing `/` are accepted but not addressable by plain path**\n`/workspace/src/routes.ts:42`\n\n`POST /docs {id: \"a/b\"}` returns 201, but `GET /docs/a/b` → 404 (it is only reachable as `/docs/a%2Fb`). Ids are also unbounded in length (a 300-char id was accepted). Not a security or data-loss issue since the storage is parameterised, but worth a format guard, e.g. reject ids not matching `/^[A-Za-z0-9._~-]{1,128}$/`.\n\n**9. `db.pragma('foreign_keys = ON')` is applied before schema creation but journal mode is left at default**\n`/workspace/src/db.ts:34-37`\n\nForeign keys are correctly enabled (verified `foreign_keys: 1`), and `ON DELETE CASCADE` works — deleting a document removed its `versions` and `active_connections` rows in my test. Durability is also fine: rows were visible from a second connection before `stop()` (`journal_mode: delete`, `synchronous: 2`). Consider `db.pragma('journal_mode = WAL')` for concurrent read throughput, but this is a tuning choice, not a correctness bug.\n\n**10. `stop()` sets `listening = false` but leaves `closed = true` permanently**\n`/workspace/src/server.ts:88-90`\n\nAfter `stop()`, `listening` is reset to `false` — implying restartability — but `closed` stays `true` and the db is closed, so a subsequent `start()` would bind a listener against a closed database and fail at the first query. Either drop the `listening = false` reset (the instance is genuinely single-use) or make `start()` throw a clear error when `closed` is true.\n\n---\n\n### Things I checked that are correct\n\n- **Express error-handler ordering** — the 404 catch-all at `/workspace/src/server.ts:35-37` placed *before* the error handler at `:38-40` is **not** a bug. Express dispatches error middleware by arity and skips non-error middleware once an error is in flight. Verified: malformed JSON body → 400 with the parse message; unknown path → 404. No change needed.\n- **SQL injection** — every statement is parameterised; the only non-parameterised query (`/workspace/src/db.ts:50`) has no interpolation.\n- **Transactions** — `insertDocument` (`db.ts:55-62`) and `commitVersion` (`db.ts:104-116`) correctly wrap multi-statement writes; no nesting problems since neither calls the other.\n- **Schema path resolution** — verified `dist/db.js` loads successfully with `cwd=/tmp`, so both the vitest (`src/`) and compiled (`dist/`) cases resolve.\n- **Persistence across restart** — documents, content and version history all survive; data is flushed without needing `stop()`.\n- **DELETE while clients connected** — cascade removes `versions` and `active_connections`, connected clients get `{type:'error', message:'Document was deleted'}` and are closed, and re-creating the same id afterwards yields a clean room (verified).\n- **PUT content overwrite** — correctly skipped when content is unchanged (no spurious version), pushes `sync` to live clients, and a stale out-of-bounds op afterwards is rejected rather than corrupting (issue 2 above is the narrower in-bounds case).\n- **`stop()` resolution** — resolves promptly (0 ms with 5 live sockets); the `!server.listening` short-circuit at `server.ts:93-96` correctly handles stop-without-start, and double-`stop()` is a safe no-op.\n\n### Recommended fix order\nIssues 1 and 2 are the ones I would block on — 1 crashes the process on any shutdown with live connections, and 2 silently corrupts document content while reporting success. Both are small, contained changes in `/workspace/src/ws.ts`."}],"totalDurationMs":201238,"totalTokens":52024,"totalToolUseCount":31,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":790,"cache_read_input_tokens":46998,"output_tokens":4234,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":790},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4234,"cache_read_input_tokens":46998,"cache_creation_input_tokens":790,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":790,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":22,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
