+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DdQvo1fuuMamNQQrEXD2gX","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Analysis complete. All findings below are empirically verified with executable reproductions.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe code is well-structured, clearly documented, and correctly implements version indexing. But the OT core has a **real TP1 convergence failure that loses data**, the broadcast protocol is **fundamentally incorrect for concurrent clients**, and there are **three confirmed uncaught-exception paths that kill the process**. The 45 passing tests never exercise true concurrency — every \"concurrent\" test serializes with `await waitForMsg(...)` or `delay()` between ops, and assertions are weak (`toContain`, `.length` checks) rather than exact-content.\n\n---\n\n## HIGH\n\n### H1. insert/delete transform loses inserted text — TP1 violation\n`/workspace/src/ot.ts:65` and `/workspace/src/ot.ts:73`\n\nBrute-forced all 63×63 op pairs on an 8-char doc: **354 TP1 failures**, of which 336 are insert/delete and delete/insert. Concrete case, doc `ABCDEFGH`, A inserts `ZZ` at 3, B deletes 5 chars at 1:\n\n```\napply(apply(doc,A), T(B,A)) = \"AGH\"     T(B,A) = {delete, position:1, count:7}\napply(apply(doc,B), T(A,B)) = \"AZZGH\"   T(A,B) = {insert, position:1, text:\"ZZ\"}\n```\n\nOrder decides whether `ZZ` survives. The cause is that line 73 (`count: op1.count + op2.text.length`) *expands the delete to swallow the concurrent insert*, while line 65 collapses the insert to the seam. These two branches disagree: one preserves the text, the other destroys it. `delete/delete` is correct (0 failures) and `insert/insert` failures are just tie-break asymmetry (see H2).\n\nFix: make the delete never consume concurrently-inserted text. Split the delete around the insertion point, or — simpler and consistent with line 65 — keep the delete's count unchanged and let the insert survive:\n\n```ts\nif (op2.type === 'insert') {\n  const deleteEnd = op1.position + op1.count;\n  if (deleteEnd <= op2.position) return { ...op1 };\n  if (op1.position >= op2.position) return { ...op1, position: op1.position + op2.text.length };\n  // Insert landed strictly inside the deleted range. Preserve it: this delete must\n  // become two ranges. Since Operation cannot express that, clamp to the prefix and\n  // let the caller apply the suffix, or model ops as a list.\n}\n```\n\nThis is the one case a single `{position,count}` delete genuinely cannot represent. The clean solution is to let `transformOperation` return `Operation | Operation[]`, or adopt a retain/insert/delete component list. Whichever you choose, the current behaviour must not silently delete text the other client just typed.\n\n### H2. Broadcasts are sent in the wrong context to clients with in-flight ops\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:195-200`\n\n`broadcast()` sends the same server-transformed operation to every client, ignoring each client's version. A client that optimistically applied its own concurrent op is in a *different* document state than the server was when it transformed. Brute force over all pairs: **1606 of 2916 pairs diverge.** Minimal case, doc `ABCDEFGH`, A inserts `1`@1, B inserts `1`@0:\n\n```\nserver:    \"1A1BCDEFGH\"\nc1 local:  \"1A1BCDEFGH\"   (ok)\nc2 local:  \"11ABCDEFGH\"   (diverged - permanent)\n```\n\nThis defeats requirement 5 (\"10 concurrent clients without corruption\"). Tests miss it because they always await the ack before the next send, so no client ever has an outstanding op.\n\nFix: track a per-connection acknowledged version, and transform the outgoing op against everything that client hasn't seen:\n\n```ts\nprivate broadcast(room: Room, exclude: WebSocket): void {\n  for (const client of room.clients) {\n    if (client === exclude) continue;\n    const seen = this.clientVersions.get(client) ?? room.version;\n    const pending = room.history.slice(seen, room.version).filter((o): o is Operation => o != null);\n    // send op rebased into *this* client's context, then advance its version\n  }\n}\n```\nStore the version alongside each socket (a `Map<WebSocket, number>` or a per-connection state object created in `handleConnection`).\n\n### H3. Three uncaught exceptions in the WS message handler crash the process\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:126` (no try/catch), `/workspace/src/ws.ts:192` (`commitVersion`)\n\n`handleMessage` is registered directly as the `'message'` listener at line 83 and contains no error handling, so any throw propagates out of the EventEmitter as an uncaught exception. All three reproduced with a `process.on('uncaughtException')` probe:\n\n1. **`FOREIGN KEY constraint failed`** — the `room` object is captured by closure at line 83, so it outlives `closeRoom()`. Any op arriving for a deleted document hits the FK on `versions.document_id`.\n2. **`UNIQUE constraint failed: versions.document_id, versions.version`** — whenever `room.version` drifts below the DB max (out-of-band writer, second process on the same SQLite file, or the `refresh()` gap in M1), `commit()` re-uses a taken version number.\n3. **`The database connection is not open`** — `stop()` closes the DB at `/workspace/src/server.ts:88` while WS frames are still buffered. Produced 2 uncaught errors in a plain `stop()`-with-op-in-flight test.\n\nNote the existing `ws.on('error', () => undefined)` at line 70 does *not* help — it only catches socket errors, not throws from the message callback.\n\nFix — wrap the handler, and re-check liveness before committing:\n\n```ts\nws.on('message', (data) => {\n  try {\n    this.handleMessage(ws, room, data.toString());\n  } catch (err) {\n    send(ws, { type: 'error', message: 'Internal error processing operation' });\n  }\n});\n```\nAdditionally: in `commit()`, bail out if `!this.db.open` or if `this.rooms.get(room.documentId) !== room`; and in `stop()`, close the DB only after the WS server has fully closed.\n\n---\n\n## MEDIUM\n\n### M1. Rebasing across a REST overwrite applies ops at stale coordinates\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:162-164`\n\nThe `.filter((op): op is Operation => op !== null)` **discards** the `null` gap markers that `refresh()` deliberately inserts at line 222 to mark untransformable REST rewrites. The op is then rebased as if the overwrite never happened. Reproduced: doc `0123456789`, REST PUT rewrites to 30 `A`s, client sends `{delete, position:0, count:10}` based on the old version — accepted and applied, silently destroying 10 characters of the new content (`AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA`).\n\nFix: treat a `null` in the range as non-transformable and reject with an error telling the client to resync:\n\n```ts\nconst window = room.history.slice(baseVersion, room.version);\nif (window.some((op) => op == null)) {\n  send(ws, { type: 'sync', documentId: room.documentId, content: room.content, version: room.version });\n  return;\n}\n```\n\n### M2. `transformAgainst` composition is sound — but only because history stores rebased ops\n`/workspace/src/ot.ts:87-93`\n\nTo answer the question directly: **the composition is valid.** `history[i]` stores the *post-transform* op (written at `/workspace/src/ws.ts:190` from the already-rebased `transformed`), so every prior op shares the context its predecessors produced. Folding left therefore keeps `result` and each `prior` in the same context at every step. I verified no out-of-bounds op is ever produced across 274k three-client permutations (0 failures), and no negative counts.\n\nTwo caveats: the fold inherits H1's non-convergence pairwise, and it depends on the invariant that `history[i]` is always the transformed form. That invariant is undocumented at the `transformAgainst` call site — worth a comment at `/workspace/src/ws.ts:190`, since storing the raw client op there instead would silently break everything.\n\n### M3. Client claiming a future version is silently accepted untransformed\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:185`\n\n`Math.min(value, room.version)` clamps a too-large version to \"up to date\", so the op is applied with **zero** transformation. A desynced client (the exact state H2 produces) gets its bad op committed verbatim rather than rejected. Fix: return `undefined` when `value > room.version` so it becomes a protocol error, or reply with a `sync`.\n\n### M4. Unicode: operations split surrogate pairs\n`/workspace/src/ot.ts:39-47`\n\nPositions are UTF-16 code units, so ops can bisect an astral character, producing lone surrogates. On `\"a😀b\"` (length 4): `{delete, position:1, count:1}` → `\"a\\ude00b\"`, and `{insert, position:2, text:\"X\"}` → `\"a\\ud83dX\\ude00b\"`. These are invalid UTF-16 and will corrupt on DB round-trip or JSON re-encode. Given the spec says \"Markdown\", emoji are realistic. Fix: reject positions that fall between a high and low surrogate in `validateOperation`, or snap them to the nearest code-point boundary.\n\n### M5. Unbounded WS payload allows OOM / `RangeError`\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:59`\n\n`express.json` is capped at 10mb (`/workspace/src/server.ts:32`) but the `WebSocketServer` uses the 100MB `ws` default with no cap on `op.text`. Repeated large inserts approach V8's ~512MB max string length; `applyOperation` then throws `RangeError: Invalid string length` inside the unguarded handler (H3). Fix: `new WebSocketServer({ server, maxPayload: 1024 * 1024 })` plus an explicit `op.text.length` limit in `validateOperation`.\n\n---\n\n## LOW\n\n- **L1 — `2**53` passes the integer bounds check.** `/workspace/src/ot.ts:12` uses `Number.isInteger`, which accepts `2**53`, where `position + count === position` due to float precision. Currently masked because the `> contentLength` check rejects it first, but it is a latent trap. Use `Number.isSafeInteger`.\n- **L2 — `latestVersion()` loads every version row to compute a max.** `/workspace/src/ws.ts:242-245` calls `listVersions` (a `SELECT *` pulling every historical content blob) just to read the last version. `getLatestVersion()` in `/workspace/src/db.ts:86` already does this with `MAX(version)` and is unused by `ws.ts`. Same issue in `loadRoom` at `/workspace/src/ws.ts:102` — select only `version, operation`.\n- **L3 — Rooms are never evicted.** `/workspace/src/ws.ts:84-87` removes the client but never deletes the room when `clients.size === 0`. Combined with the ever-growing `history` array, memory grows unboundedly with documents touched.\n- **L4 — No-op operations commit a version.** `{insert, text:\"\"}` and `{delete, count:0}` pass validation and produce a version row plus a broadcast. Note H1's transform legitimately generates `count:0` deletes (330 of the pairs tested), so these will occur naturally. Consider short-circuiting in `commit()`.\n- **L5 — Express error handler can double-send.** `/workspace/src/server.ts:38-40` ignores whether `res.headersSent` is already true, which throws `ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT` for errors raised mid-response. Guard with `if (res.headersSent) return _next(err);`.\n- **L6 — `stop()` swallows `server.close()` errors** and `wss.close(() => undefined)` discards its callback error, so shutdown failures are invisible.\n\n---\n\n## What was done well\n\nVersion/history indexing is genuinely correct — I specifically hunted for the off-by-one and there isn't one. `history[v] = op taking v → v+1` holds consistently across `commit()` (`/workspace/src/ws.ts:190-191`), `loadRoom()`'s `history[row.version - 1]` (line 114), and the `slice(baseVersion, room.version)` window (line 162). `delete/delete` transform is correct (0 TP1 failures across all pairs), including the tricky partial-overlap `removedBefore`/`overlap` arithmetic. DB writes are properly wrapped in `db.transaction()`, `applyOperation` defensively clamps, the socket-tracking shutdown logic avoids keep-alive hangs, and the `RoomSync` interface is a clean seam that keeps `routes.ts` testable without the WS layer.\n\n## Recommended priority\n\nFix **H3** first — it is a small, self-contained change that stops the process from dying. Then **H1** and **H2** together, since both require deciding on the operation representation (a retain/insert/delete component list would resolve H1 cleanly and make per-client rebasing in H2 straightforward). **M1** and **M3** are quick follow-ups once per-client version tracking from H2 exists.\n\nOne caveat on scope: H1 and H2 are architectural, not one-line patches, and the existing test suite will not catch regressions in either. I'd recommend adding a randomized TP1 property test over op pairs and a simulated multi-client convergence harness before attempting the fix — otherwise there's no signal that it worked."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a603c5317d7836b4d (use SendMessage with to: 'a603c5317d7836b4d' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 61382\ntool_uses: 26\nduration_ms: 293951</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e246dbcb-e9b4-4717-8ce2-8446dd5eb5a5","uuid":"895f3304-6f5d-41fc-bdc2-ef21f4e5d8ea","timestamp":"2026-07-25T01:24:09.955Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Review the implementation in /workspace/src/ (files: db.ts, models.ts, ot.ts, routes.ts, ws.ts, server.ts) against the spec in /workspace/TASK.md and the tests in /workspace/tests/collab.test.ts.\n\nFocus specifically on CORRECTNESS of the operational transform logic in src/ot.ts and the WebSocket room/version management in src/ws.ts:\n- Are the four transform cases (insert/insert, insert/delete, delete/insert, delete/delete) correct? Check TP1 convergence property with concrete examples.\n- Is `transformAgainst` (sequential transform against a list of prior ops) sound? Note the server has a single linear history so each prior op was itself already rebased onto the ones before it — verify this composition is actually valid.\n- Version tracking, history array indexing (history[v] = op taking v -> v+1), off-by-one errors.\n- Race conditions / state corruption with 10 concurrent clients.\n- Bounds validation, negative counts, integer overflow, unicode/surrogate-pair issues.\n- Error handling paths that could crash the process (unhandled exceptions in ws message handlers, db errors).\n\nDo NOT run the test suite (it already passes, 45/45). Read the code carefully and reason about edge cases the tests do not cover. Report findings as HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW priority with file:line references and concrete recommended fixes. Be specific and skeptical; do not invent problems that aren't real.","agentId":"a603c5317d7836b4d","agentType":"conclave:code-reviewer","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Analysis complete. All findings below are empirically verified with executable reproductions.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe code is well-structured, clearly documented, and correctly implements version indexing. But the OT core has a **real TP1 convergence failure that loses data**, the broadcast protocol is **fundamentally incorrect for concurrent clients**, and there are **three confirmed uncaught-exception paths that kill the process**. The 45 passing tests never exercise true concurrency — every \"concurrent\" test serializes with `await waitForMsg(...)` or `delay()` between ops, and assertions are weak (`toContain`, `.length` checks) rather than exact-content.\n\n---\n\n## HIGH\n\n### H1. insert/delete transform loses inserted text — TP1 violation\n`/workspace/src/ot.ts:65` and `/workspace/src/ot.ts:73`\n\nBrute-forced all 63×63 op pairs on an 8-char doc: **354 TP1 failures**, of which 336 are insert/delete and delete/insert. Concrete case, doc `ABCDEFGH`, A inserts `ZZ` at 3, B deletes 5 chars at 1:\n\n```\napply(apply(doc,A), T(B,A)) = \"AGH\"     T(B,A) = {delete, position:1, count:7}\napply(apply(doc,B), T(A,B)) = \"AZZGH\"   T(A,B) = {insert, position:1, text:\"ZZ\"}\n```\n\nOrder decides whether `ZZ` survives. The cause is that line 73 (`count: op1.count + op2.text.length`) *expands the delete to swallow the concurrent insert*, while line 65 collapses the insert to the seam. These two branches disagree: one preserves the text, the other destroys it. `delete/delete` is correct (0 failures) and `insert/insert` failures are just tie-break asymmetry (see H2).\n\nFix: make the delete never consume concurrently-inserted text. Split the delete around the insertion point, or — simpler and consistent with line 65 — keep the delete's count unchanged and let the insert survive:\n\n```ts\nif (op2.type === 'insert') {\n  const deleteEnd = op1.position + op1.count;\n  if (deleteEnd <= op2.position) return { ...op1 };\n  if (op1.position >= op2.position) return { ...op1, position: op1.position + op2.text.length };\n  // Insert landed strictly inside the deleted range. Preserve it: this delete must\n  // become two ranges. Since Operation cannot express that, clamp to the prefix and\n  // let the caller apply the suffix, or model ops as a list.\n}\n```\n\nThis is the one case a single `{position,count}` delete genuinely cannot represent. The clean solution is to let `transformOperation` return `Operation | Operation[]`, or adopt a retain/insert/delete component list. Whichever you choose, the current behaviour must not silently delete text the other client just typed.\n\n### H2. Broadcasts are sent in the wrong context to clients with in-flight ops\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:195-200`\n\n`broadcast()` sends the same server-transformed operation to every client, ignoring each client's version. A client that optimistically applied its own concurrent op is in a *different* document state than the server was when it transformed. Brute force over all pairs: **1606 of 2916 pairs diverge.** Minimal case, doc `ABCDEFGH`, A inserts `1`@1, B inserts `1`@0:\n\n```\nserver:    \"1A1BCDEFGH\"\nc1 local:  \"1A1BCDEFGH\"   (ok)\nc2 local:  \"11ABCDEFGH\"   (diverged - permanent)\n```\n\nThis defeats requirement 5 (\"10 concurrent clients without corruption\"). Tests miss it because they always await the ack before the next send, so no client ever has an outstanding op.\n\nFix: track a per-connection acknowledged version, and transform the outgoing op against everything that client hasn't seen:\n\n```ts\nprivate broadcast(room: Room, exclude: WebSocket): void {\n  for (const client of room.clients) {\n    if (client === exclude) continue;\n    const seen = this.clientVersions.get(client) ?? room.version;\n    const pending = room.history.slice(seen, room.version).filter((o): o is Operation => o != null);\n    // send op rebased into *this* client's context, then advance its version\n  }\n}\n```\nStore the version alongside each socket (a `Map<WebSocket, number>` or a per-connection state object created in `handleConnection`).\n\n### H3. Three uncaught exceptions in the WS message handler crash the process\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:126` (no try/catch), `/workspace/src/ws.ts:192` (`commitVersion`)\n\n`handleMessage` is registered directly as the `'message'` listener at line 83 and contains no error handling, so any throw propagates out of the EventEmitter as an uncaught exception. All three reproduced with a `process.on('uncaughtException')` probe:\n\n1. **`FOREIGN KEY constraint failed`** — the `room` object is captured by closure at line 83, so it outlives `closeRoom()`. Any op arriving for a deleted document hits the FK on `versions.document_id`.\n2. **`UNIQUE constraint failed: versions.document_id, versions.version`** — whenever `room.version` drifts below the DB max (out-of-band writer, second process on the same SQLite file, or the `refresh()` gap in M1), `commit()` re-uses a taken version number.\n3. **`The database connection is not open`** — `stop()` closes the DB at `/workspace/src/server.ts:88` while WS frames are still buffered. Produced 2 uncaught errors in a plain `stop()`-with-op-in-flight test.\n\nNote the existing `ws.on('error', () => undefined)` at line 70 does *not* help — it only catches socket errors, not throws from the message callback.\n\nFix — wrap the handler, and re-check liveness before committing:\n\n```ts\nws.on('message', (data) => {\n  try {\n    this.handleMessage(ws, room, data.toString());\n  } catch (err) {\n    send(ws, { type: 'error', message: 'Internal error processing operation' });\n  }\n});\n```\nAdditionally: in `commit()`, bail out if `!this.db.open` or if `this.rooms.get(room.documentId) !== room`; and in `stop()`, close the DB only after the WS server has fully closed.\n\n---\n\n## MEDIUM\n\n### M1. Rebasing across a REST overwrite applies ops at stale coordinates\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:162-164`\n\nThe `.filter((op): op is Operation => op !== null)` **discards** the `null` gap markers that `refresh()` deliberately inserts at line 222 to mark untransformable REST rewrites. The op is then rebased as if the overwrite never happened. Reproduced: doc `0123456789`, REST PUT rewrites to 30 `A`s, client sends `{delete, position:0, count:10}` based on the old version — accepted and applied, silently destroying 10 characters of the new content (`AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA`).\n\nFix: treat a `null` in the range as non-transformable and reject with an error telling the client to resync:\n\n```ts\nconst window = room.history.slice(baseVersion, room.version);\nif (window.some((op) => op == null)) {\n  send(ws, { type: 'sync', documentId: room.documentId, content: room.content, version: room.version });\n  return;\n}\n```\n\n### M2. `transformAgainst` composition is sound — but only because history stores rebased ops\n`/workspace/src/ot.ts:87-93`\n\nTo answer the question directly: **the composition is valid.** `history[i]` stores the *post-transform* op (written at `/workspace/src/ws.ts:190` from the already-rebased `transformed`), so every prior op shares the context its predecessors produced. Folding left therefore keeps `result` and each `prior` in the same context at every step. I verified no out-of-bounds op is ever produced across 274k three-client permutations (0 failures), and no negative counts.\n\nTwo caveats: the fold inherits H1's non-convergence pairwise, and it depends on the invariant that `history[i]` is always the transformed form. That invariant is undocumented at the `transformAgainst` call site — worth a comment at `/workspace/src/ws.ts:190`, since storing the raw client op there instead would silently break everything.\n\n### M3. Client claiming a future version is silently accepted untransformed\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:185`\n\n`Math.min(value, room.version)` clamps a too-large version to \"up to date\", so the op is applied with **zero** transformation. A desynced client (the exact state H2 produces) gets its bad op committed verbatim rather than rejected. Fix: return `undefined` when `value > room.version` so it becomes a protocol error, or reply with a `sync`.\n\n### M4. Unicode: operations split surrogate pairs\n`/workspace/src/ot.ts:39-47`\n\nPositions are UTF-16 code units, so ops can bisect an astral character, producing lone surrogates. On `\"a😀b\"` (length 4): `{delete, position:1, count:1}` → `\"a\\ude00b\"`, and `{insert, position:2, text:\"X\"}` → `\"a\\ud83dX\\ude00b\"`. These are invalid UTF-16 and will corrupt on DB round-trip or JSON re-encode. Given the spec says \"Markdown\", emoji are realistic. Fix: reject positions that fall between a high and low surrogate in `validateOperation`, or snap them to the nearest code-point boundary.\n\n### M5. Unbounded WS payload allows OOM / `RangeError`\n`/workspace/src/ws.ts:59`\n\n`express.json` is capped at 10mb (`/workspace/src/server.ts:32`) but the `WebSocketServer` uses the 100MB `ws` default with no cap on `op.text`. Repeated large inserts approach V8's ~512MB max string length; `applyOperation` then throws `RangeError: Invalid string length` inside the unguarded handler (H3). Fix: `new WebSocketServer({ server, maxPayload: 1024 * 1024 })` plus an explicit `op.text.length` limit in `validateOperation`.\n\n---\n\n## LOW\n\n- **L1 — `2**53` passes the integer bounds check.** `/workspace/src/ot.ts:12` uses `Number.isInteger`, which accepts `2**53`, where `position + count === position` due to float precision. Currently masked because the `> contentLength` check rejects it first, but it is a latent trap. Use `Number.isSafeInteger`.\n- **L2 — `latestVersion()` loads every version row to compute a max.** `/workspace/src/ws.ts:242-245` calls `listVersions` (a `SELECT *` pulling every historical content blob) just to read the last version. `getLatestVersion()` in `/workspace/src/db.ts:86` already does this with `MAX(version)` and is unused by `ws.ts`. Same issue in `loadRoom` at `/workspace/src/ws.ts:102` — select only `version, operation`.\n- **L3 — Rooms are never evicted.** `/workspace/src/ws.ts:84-87` removes the client but never deletes the room when `clients.size === 0`. Combined with the ever-growing `history` array, memory grows unboundedly with documents touched.\n- **L4 — No-op operations commit a version.** `{insert, text:\"\"}` and `{delete, count:0}` pass validation and produce a version row plus a broadcast. Note H1's transform legitimately generates `count:0` deletes (330 of the pairs tested), so these will occur naturally. Consider short-circuiting in `commit()`.\n- **L5 — Express error handler can double-send.** `/workspace/src/server.ts:38-40` ignores whether `res.headersSent` is already true, which throws `ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT` for errors raised mid-response. Guard with `if (res.headersSent) return _next(err);`.\n- **L6 — `stop()` swallows `server.close()` errors** and `wss.close(() => undefined)` discards its callback error, so shutdown failures are invisible.\n\n---\n\n## What was done well\n\nVersion/history indexing is genuinely correct — I specifically hunted for the off-by-one and there isn't one. `history[v] = op taking v → v+1` holds consistently across `commit()` (`/workspace/src/ws.ts:190-191`), `loadRoom()`'s `history[row.version - 1]` (line 114), and the `slice(baseVersion, room.version)` window (line 162). `delete/delete` transform is correct (0 TP1 failures across all pairs), including the tricky partial-overlap `removedBefore`/`overlap` arithmetic. DB writes are properly wrapped in `db.transaction()`, `applyOperation` defensively clamps, the socket-tracking shutdown logic avoids keep-alive hangs, and the `RoomSync` interface is a clean seam that keeps `routes.ts` testable without the WS layer.\n\n## Recommended priority\n\nFix **H3** first — it is a small, self-contained change that stops the process from dying. Then **H1** and **H2** together, since both require deciding on the operation representation (a retain/insert/delete component list would resolve H1 cleanly and make per-client rebasing in H2 straightforward). **M1** and **M3** are quick follow-ups once per-client version tracking from H2 exists.\n\nOne caveat on scope: H1 and H2 are architectural, not one-line patches, and the existing test suite will not catch regressions in either. I'd recommend adding a randomized TP1 property test over op pairs and a simulated multi-client convergence harness before attempting the fix — otherwise there's no signal that it worked."}],"totalDurationMs":293951,"totalTokens":61382,"totalToolUseCount":26,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1201,"cache_read_input_tokens":55522,"output_tokens":4657,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1201},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4657,"cache_read_input_tokens":55522,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":17,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
